 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians and training views. The use of pretrained priors accelerates convergence, reducing the total number of steps required compared to training from scratch [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The initial feed-forward process may generate redundant Gaussians, but pruning during fine-tuning significantly reduces the final number of primitives, lowering memory consumption [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves photorealism on par with or surpassing other methods, particularly in urban view synthesis [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Accelerated training, reduced memory consumption through pruning, high fidelity [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial feed-forward initialization or pretrained weights [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When starting from a pretrained model or a coarse initialization and aiming for high-fidelity results with efficient memory usage [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When no pretrained weights are available and the initial point cloud is extremely sparse or noisy, potentially requiring more robust initialization strategies [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians and the cost of differentiable rasterization. Fine-tuning accelerates convergence compared to test-time optimization methods due to pretrained priors [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Significantly reduced during fine-tuning as redundant initial Gaussians are pruned, maintaining high fidelity with lower memory consumption [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves photorealism on par with or surpassing other methods [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast convergence, memory efficiency through pruning, high fidelity.
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial feed-forward prediction or SfM points.
+*   **When to use:** When a high-quality initial representation is available and memory efficiency is a concern.
+*   **When not to use:** When the initial initialization is poor, as fine-tuning may not recover from significant structural errors.
 
 ## Background
-Fine-tuning in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting is a critical phase in pipelines that utilize pretrained models or feed-forward initialization. It was notably employed in EVolSplat, a method for efficient volume-based Gaussian Splatting for urban view synthesis, where it leveraged powerful pretrained weights to achieve photorealism [S1]. The approach builds on the vanilla 3DGS optimization framework but integrates densification and pruning to manage the population of Gaussians effectively [S1].
+Fine-tuning in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced in **EVolSplat: Efficient Volume-based Gaussian Splatting for Urban View Synthesis** [S1]. The method leverages pretrained weights to accelerate network training and enable faster convergence compared to other test-time optimization methods. It addresses the inefficiency of standard 3DGS initialization by dynamically managing the Gaussian population during the optimization phase.
 
